virtiofs: the notification queue, with inotify for what the host changes

The virtio spec's VIRTIO_FS_F_NOTIFICATION, which Linux never took: virtqueue 1 holds buffers
the device fills with FUSE notifications, the request queues follow it. lodka sends them for
what changed on the Mac (FSEvents): INVAL_INODE for a file's contents or attributes,
INVAL_ENTRY for a name made, DELETE for a name removed, INC_EPOCH when it lost track. Each drops
what the guest cached, as the /dev/fuse notifications do, and tells fsnotify, so inotify
watchers in the guest see changes made on the Mac. INC_EPOCH drops every inode's attributes,
pages and listing too, which /dev/fuse's leaves: an open file looks up no name. INVAL_INODE and
INC_EPOCH count as an evict too: an inode a LOOKUP or READDIRPLUS reply on its way makes, with
attributes from before the change, takes them as stale. The guest can then cache names and
attributes for long.

Three flags of lodka's own. VIRTIO_FS_INVAL_MODIFIED on an INVAL_ENTRY, for a file the guest
never looked up whose contents changed, has the guest look it up if inotify watches its
directory, and tell inotify. VIRTIO_FS_INVAL_DIR on an INVAL_ENTRY, or in a DELETE's padding,
says the name is a directory's, for IN_ISDIR: the guest has no inode for a name just made or
removed. FOPEN_INVAL_ATTR on an open, for a file changed since the guest last got its
attributes, has them fetched there: a notification still on its way would leave the old size,
and a splice reads up to it.
